  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nThis Afternoon through Evening: Severe Storm Potential\n\nA few isolated showers and storms remain across the forecast area \nlate this morning, mainly across the southern half of the area. \nThese storms are not in a prime environment to become severe at this \npoint, but that will change as we go into the afternoon.\n\nCloud cover has become much more sparse than previously forecast for \nthis morning, which is allowing temperatures to rise into the 70s, \nwith some upper 70s already being observed. This is in part due to \nthe rain shower/storm coverage from overnight not being as \nwidespread as model guidance was hinting at yesterday. With that \nbeing said, the severe storm environment for this afternoon is \nincreasing. A weak surface front, with a north wind in its wake, is \nforecast to move in over the area in the next few hours. The front \nshould set up near I-90 by early to mid afternoon and this front is \nexpected to be the focal point for additional thunderstorm \ndevelopment beginning in the 1-3 PM window. Storms should form along \nthe boundary and move off to the east/southeast this afternoon into \nthis evening. Sufficient severe storm ingredients will be in place \nat that time, with MLCAPE values of 1500-2000 J/kg and bulk shear \nupwards of 40-50 kts. Isolated development of storms is expected \ninitially, with a threat for large hail and damaging wind gusts. The \nstorms may congeal and form a linear line at some point this \nevening, just when or if that happens is uncertain and that is \nwhen the main severe threat would turn to a damaging wind gust \nthreat. The tornado potential is very low, but with a surface \nboundary in play, a tornado or two cannot be completely ruled \nout. Storms should begin to exit off to the east/southeast early\nthis evening, but a few may linger into the 9-11 PM time frame \nwith the severe threat decreasing by that point.\n\nWednesday through Friday Night: Periodic Rain Chances\n\nThe forecast area should be in between two broad systems to end this \nweek with a trough to the east and a building ridge to the west. \nThis pattern will lead to northwest flow over the Upper Midwest \nduring the later half of this work week and temperatures will remain \nnear average in the upper 70s to low 80s each afternoon. The next \ndecent chance for showers and storms is expected to be Thursday \nnight into Friday morning as a H500 impulse moves over the region. \nThe better chances for a half inch of rain or more are across \nnortheastern Minnesota into northern Wisconsin, which are currently \ndealing with drought conditions and would certainly benefit from\nany rainfall that the system can provide. Some rain showers or \nstorms may linger into the day on Friday, mainly across the \neastern half of the forecast area.\n\n\nThis Weekend into Next Week: Generally Dry Conditions with Mild \nTemperatures\n\nThe upper level pattern in the forecast guidance diverges beginning \nSaturday through the remainder of this forecast period. There is \nfairly good agreement that a trough will dig south over the eastern \nCONUS as the ridge from the west moves slightly east over the \nPlains. Northerly to northwesterly flow will remain over the region \nduring this time frame, but that will be dependent upon where each \nfeature exactly sets up. Rain chances may continue if mid level \nmoisture can remain far enough east over the Upper Midwest, but \nmostly dry conditions are favored at this time. Temperatures will \ncontinue to remain in the 70s to mid 80s.",
